{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/74593-so-i-finally-visited-arcosanti","id":74593,"title":"So, I finally visited Arcosanti...","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Trinity Continuum"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-02-18T15:19:00-08:00","id":74593,"title":"So, I finally visited Arcosanti...","content":"I was first introduced to this place it in the original Aeon book as a reference for Arcologies and have wanted to visit ever since, I can now finally cross this one off the bucket list.<br /> <br /> I had a trip to Arizona last week and managed to swing by Arcosanti and spend a few hours there. With it being off-season for tourists we got quite the tour, including looking through the Soleri art / architectural drawings from the last few decades that they were in the process of archiving, seeing the underground maintenance tunnels etc. While it definitely had that hippie / commune vibe going for it (which may or may not impact any visitors impressions), we had a great time, just rolled with it and bombarded our poor tour with tons of questions. The guide was knowledgeable and his enthusiasm was quite infectious. These guys love and, I mean <i><b>love,</b></i> what they're doing there.<br /> <br /> Its a fascinating place; and I came away with a distinct impression of...well, 'controlled chaos' really. The description they give it of being an 'architectural laboratory' is on the mark.<br /> <br /> Its well worth a visit to get a better handle on what could be achieved in terms of Arcologies, and definitely spend a lot of time with the guide(s) - they really know their stuff.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#ff0000\"><b>Ad Astra</b></span>","edited":{"userid":232,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/232-chill","user":"CHILL","date":". 02-19-2014, 10:53 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/memb